{{also|dispensé}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|dispence}} {{q|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|-}}, from {{der|en|fro|dispenser}}, from {{der|en|la|dispenso|dispēnsāre|to weigh out, pay out, distribute, regulate, manage, control, dispense}}, frequentative of {{m|la|dispendo|dispendere|to weigh out}}, from {{m|la|dis-||apart}} + {{m|la|pendo|pendere|to weigh}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈspɛns/|a=RP,GA,CA}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-dispense.ogg|a=California}}
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈspens/|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛns|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|dis|pense}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[issue]], [[distribute]], or give out.
#* {{RQ:Scott Guy Mannering|passage=He is delighted to '''dispense''' a share of it to all the company.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1955|author=William Golding|title=The Inheritors|publisher=Faber and Faber|year_published=2005|page=40
|passage=The smoky spray seemed to trap whatever light there was and to '''dispense''' it subtly.}}
# To apply, as laws to particular cases; to administer; to execute; to manage; to direct.
#: ''to '''dispense''' justice''
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1662|author=w:John Dryden|title=To the Lord Chancellor Hyde
|passage=While you '''dispense''' the laws, and guide the state.}}
# To [[supply]] or [[make up]] a medicine or prescription.
#: {{ux|en|The pharmacist '''dispensed''' my tablets.}}
#: {{ux|en|An optician can '''dispense''' spectacles.}}
# {{lb|en|particularly|_|in|_|canon law}} To give a [[dispensation]] to (someone); to [[excuse]].
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=34|book=II|passage=After his victories, he often gave them the reines to all licenciousnesse, for a while '''dispencing''' them from all rules of military discipline{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1643|author=w:John Milton|title=Doctrine and Discipline of Divorce
|passage=Of evils the first and greatest is, that hereby a most absurd and rash imputation is fixt upon God and his holy Laws, of conniving and '''dispensing''' with open and common adultery among his chosen people; a thing which the rankest politician would think it shame and disworship, that his Laws should countenance; how and in what manner this comes to passe, I shall reserve, till the course of method brings on the unfolding of many Scriptures.}}
#* '''1779–81''', {{w|Samuel Johnson}}, "Richard Savage" in ''Lives of the Most Eminent English Poet''
#*: He appeared to think himself born to be supported by others, and '''dispensed''' from all necessity of providing for himself.
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|chapter=11|passage=It was resolved that all members of the House who held commissions, should be '''dispensed''' from parliamentary attendance.}}
#: {{ux|en|Every spring, the passive aggressive archbishop suffered from a brief flu that '''dispensed''' him '''from''' needing to provide Easter service to the king.}}
#: {{ux|en|She was '''dispensed from''' her vows and married Captain von Trapp.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To [[compensate]]; to make up; to make amends.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=I|canto=III|stanza=30|passage=One loving howre / For many yeares of sorrow can '''dispence'''}}
#* {{RQ:Gower CA|passage=His synne was '''dispensed''' with golde, wherof it was compensed}}

